{%- liquid
  comment
    VALUE RESOLUTION — ORCHESTRATOR EXTENSION POINT.
    custom.fit_scale is the explicit override and must keep winning. Extend only
    the blank fallback branch below when deriving a value from custom.fit_note.
  endcomment
  capture fit_override
    echo product.metafields.custom.fit_scale.value | default: product.metafields.custom.fit_scale
  endcapture
  assign fit_resolved = fit_override | strip
  if fit_resolved == blank
    comment
      DERIVED FROM custom.fit_note — no separate metafield run needed.

      custom.fit_note is already populated across the catalog in a consistent voice by an
      existing product-content pipeline, and each note states the sizing verdict in plain
      words. So the scale reads the data that already exists, and custom.fit_scale stays
      available as a per-product override for anything this gets wrong.

      ORDER IS THE WHOLE TRICK. "true to size" is tested BEFORE "size down", because
      "Runs true to size; size down for a closer fit" is a TRUE-TO-SIZE garment with a
      styling tip attached — not a garment that runs large. Testing "size down" first
      would mis-sort every one of those. Ordering the branches this way also avoids
      needing a negation, which Liquid cannot express cleanly (`contains` does not
      combine with `== false`).

      Deliberately conservative: it fires only on an explicit statement and renders
      NOTHING otherwise. A blank scale is honest; a guessed one sends someone the wrong
      size and the parcel comes back.

      Worked examples:
        "Runs 1-2 sizes small, size up."                       -> runs-small
        "Runs small, size up for a relaxed fit."               -> runs-small
        "Runs true to size; size down for a closer fit."       -> true-to-size
        "Runs oversized, order your usual size."               -> true-to-size
        "Relaxed through the shoulders, order your usual size" -> true-to-size
        "Runs oversized by design, size down for a closer fit" -> runs-large
        "Runs loose and relaxed, size down for a closer fit."  -> runs-large
        "Oversized, relaxed fit; size up for an even roomier"  -> nothing
    endcomment
    capture fit_note_raw
      echo product.metafields.custom.fit_note.value | default: product.metafields.custom.fit_note
    endcapture
    comment
      custom.fit_note may be written in Danish rather than English (e.g.
      "Passer i normal størrelse"), so Danish patterns are matched alongside
      the English ones. Branch ORDER still matters — see the long comment
      above; the Danish phrases follow the same logic: an explicit
      "størrelse større" (order a size up) marks runs-small BEFORE the
      true-to-size test, "størrelse mindre" marks runs-large after it.
    endcomment
    assign fit_note = fit_note_raw | downcase
    assign fit_derived = ''
    comment
      Intermediate positions: "lidt lille/stor" & "slightly small/large" land
      BETWEEN the poles (25% / 75%) — these checks must run FIRST, because
      "lidt lille i størrelsen" also contains the hard "lille i størrelsen"
      needle and would otherwise mis-sort to the pole.
    endcomment
    if fit_note contains 'lidt lille' or fit_note contains 'en anelse lille' or fit_note contains 'slightly small' or fit_note contains 'a bit small' or fit_note contains 'runs a little small'
      assign fit_derived = 'slightly-small'
    elsif fit_note contains 'lidt stor' or fit_note contains 'en anelse stor' or fit_note contains 'slightly large' or fit_note contains 'a bit large' or fit_note contains 'runs a little large'
      assign fit_derived = 'slightly-large'
    elsif fit_note contains 'runs small' or fit_note contains 'sizes small' or fit_note contains 'lille i størrelsen' or fit_note contains 'størrelse større' or fit_note contains 'str. større'
      assign fit_derived = 'runs-small'
    elsif fit_note contains 'true to size' or fit_note contains 'true-to-size' or fit_note contains 'order your usual size' or fit_note contains 'fits true' or fit_note contains 'normal størrelse' or fit_note contains 'normal i størrelsen' or fit_note contains 'størrelsessvarende' or fit_note contains 'sædvanlige størrelse'
      comment
        "normal størrelse"/true-to-size notes are often generic boilerplate
        rather than verified fit data — nearly the whole catalogue can carry
        them, which would pin the scale at 50% storewide if trusted directly.
        They fall through to the 25% catalogue default below instead. The
        branch itself MUST stay: it still absorbs "runs true to size; size
        down for a closer fit" before the size-down needle mis-sorts it to
        runs-large. A real 50% comes only from an explicit custom.fit_scale
        override backed by verified sizing data.
      endcomment
      assign fit_derived = 'slightly-small'
    elsif fit_note contains 'size down' or fit_note contains 'stor i størrelsen' or fit_note contains 'størrelse mindre' or fit_note contains 'str. mindre'
      assign fit_derived = 'runs-large'
    endif
    comment
      DEFAULT: products with no derivable note show the marker at 25%
      (slightly-small) instead of rendering nothing — the scale appears on
      EVERY product with zero data upkeep. This deliberately overrides a
      stricter "blank is honest" default: slightly-small is treated as the
      safest catalogue-wide statement when sizing tends to run small more
      often than large, and it nudges toward the size chart. An explicit
      fit_note or custom.fit_scale still wins the moment it exists.
    endcomment
    if fit_derived == ''
      assign fit_derived = 'slightly-small'
    endif
    assign fit_resolved = fit_derived
  endif

  assign fit_key = fit_resolved | downcase | strip | replace: '_', '-' | replace: ' ', '-'
  assign fit_key = fit_key | replace: '--', '-' | replace: '--', '-'
  assign fit_position = ''
  assign fit_label = ''
  case fit_key
    when 'runs-small'
      assign fit_position = '0%'
      assign fit_label = 'product.fit.runs_small' | t
    when 'slightly-small'
      assign fit_position = '25%'
      assign fit_label = 'product.fit.slightly_small' | t
    when 'true-to-size'
      assign fit_position = '50%'
      assign fit_label = 'product.fit.true_to_size_label' | t
    when 'slightly-large'
      assign fit_position = '75%'
      assign fit_label = 'product.fit.slightly_large' | t
    when 'runs-large'
      assign fit_position = '100%'
      assign fit_label = 'product.fit.runs_large' | t
  endcase
-%}
{%- if fit_position != blank -%}
  <div class="pdp-fit" style="--fit-position: {{ fit_position }}" aria-label="{{ 'product.fit.heading' | t }}: {{ fit_label | escape }}">
    <p class="pdp-fit__heading lbl-sm">{{ 'product.fit.heading' | t }}</p>
    <div class="pdp-fit__track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="pdp-fit__marker"></span></div>
    <div class="pdp-fit__labels stmt-sm mute" aria-hidden="true">
      <span>{{ 'product.fit.runs_small' | t }}</span>
      <span>{{ 'product.fit.true_to_size_label' | t }}</span>
      <span>{{ 'product.fit.runs_large' | t }}</span>
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
